Find the area between $y=x^3+2$ and the $y$-axis, from the height of the curve at $x=0$ to its height at $x=3$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.3cm,y=0.12cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{regionfill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[regionfill] (0,2)--plot[domain=0:3,variable=\t,samples=80] ({\t},{\t*\t*\t+2})--(0,29)--cycle;
\draw[black,->] (-0.4,0)--(3.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,36) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,->] plot[domain=-0.8:3.15,variable=\t,samples=80] ({\t},{\t*\t*\t+2});
\draw[black] (0,29)--(3,29);
\draw[black,dashed] (3,0)--(3,29);
\path (3,0) node[below] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Rearrange to $x=\sqrt[3]{y-2}$. When $x=0$, $y=2$; when $x=3$, $y=29$.
$\begin{align*} A&=\int_2^{29}(y-2)^{1/3}\,dy\\&=\left[\frac{3}{4}(y-2)^{4/3}\right]_2^{29}\\&=\frac{3}{4}(81-0)\\&=\frac{243}{4}\ \text{units}^2\end{align*}$
The converted bounds and corresponding points are shown below.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.3cm,y=0.12cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{regionfill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[regionfill] (0,2)--plot[domain=0:3,variable=\t,samples=80] ({\t},{\t*\t*\t+2})--(0,29)--cycle;
\draw[black,->] (-0.4,0)--(3.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,36) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,->] plot[domain=-0.8:3.15,variable=\t,samples=80] ({\t},{\t*\t*\t+2});
\draw[black] (0,29)--(3,29);
\draw[black,dashed] (3,0)--(3,29);
\path (0,2) node[left] {$2$} (0,29) node[left] {$29$} (3,0) node[below] {$3$} (3,29) node[above right] {$(3,29)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Find the total area bounded by $x=y(y-4)$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=6$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.32cm,y=0.65cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{regionfill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[regionfill] (0,0)--plot[domain=0:4,variable=\t,samples=60] ({\t*(\t-4)},{\t})--cycle;
\fill[regionfill] (0,4)--plot[domain=4:6,variable=\t,samples=60] ({\t*(\t-4)},{\t})--(0,6)--cycle;
\draw[black,->] (-5.5,0)--(15,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.5)--(0,7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,->] plot[domain=-0.3:6.2,variable=\t,samples=80] ({\t*(\t-4)},{\t});
\draw[black] (0,6)--(12,6);
\path (0,6) node[left] {$6$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Find where the curve meets the $y$-axis.
$\begin{align*} y(y-4)&=0\\y&=0\ \text{or}\ 4\end{align*}$
The curve lies left of the axis for $0<y<4$ and right for $4<y\leq6$. Split at $y=4$ and add the positive areas.
$\begin{align*} A&=-\int_0^4(y^2-4y)\,dy+\int_4^6(y^2-4y)\,dy\\&=-\left[\frac{y^3}{3}-2y^2\right]_0^4+\left[\frac{y^3}{3}-2y^2\right]_4^6\\&=-\left(-\frac{32}{3}\right)+\left(0+\frac{32}{3}\right)\\&=\frac{64}{3}\ \text{units}^2\end{align*}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.32cm,y=0.65cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{regionfill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[regionfill] (0,0)--plot[domain=0:4,variable=\t,samples=60] ({\t*(\t-4)},{\t})--cycle;
\fill[regionfill] (0,4)--plot[domain=4:6,variable=\t,samples=60] ({\t*(\t-4)},{\t})--(0,6)--cycle;
\draw[black,->] (-5.5,0)--(15,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.5)--(0,7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,->] plot[domain=-0.3:6.2,variable=\t,samples=80] ({\t*(\t-4)},{\t});
\draw[black] (0,6)--(12,6);
\fill[black] (0,4) circle[radius=1.5pt];
\path (0,4) node[left] {$4$} (0,6) node[left] {$6$} (-4,2) node[left] {Left} (7,5.4) node[right] {Right};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
